India has black sand too, though not mostly from volcanoes. Along parts of the Kerala coast near Kollam, such as Chavara and Neendakara, rivers and waves have gathered heavy minerals washed out of the Western Ghats. Because these grains are heavier than quartz, the waves wash away the lighter sand and leave dark streaks behind.

These sands contain ilmenite, used to make titanium for paint, aircraft and rocket parts, and monazite, which holds thorium, a possible nuclear fuel. A government company, Indian Rare Earths Limited, has mined them for decades. Mining has also worn away parts of the coast, and some villages nearby have protested that the sea now reaches closer to their homes. Further north, near Karwar in Karnataka, Tilmati Beach has small coves of dark sand that come from local volcanic rock.

{{story:waves-how}}

At the centre of our Milky Way is a black hole called Sagittarius A*, about four million times heavier than the Sun. Its event horizon lies about 12 million kilometres from its centre, roughly 30 times the distance from Earth to the Moon.

Because the edge is so far out, the stretching there is mild. You could, in theory, drift across it and feel nothing special at that moment. The universe behind you would look bent and brightened as light from outside piled in. The stretching would come later, as you fell deeper.

{{story:einstein}}

In spring and summer, the koel's loud, rising "ku-oo, ku-oo" can go on for hours. It is the male calling. He is glossy black with red eyes. The female is brown with white spots.

Koels don't build nests. The female lays her eggs in a crow's nest, often while the male distracts the crows. The crows hatch the koel chicks and feed them as their own. This trick, called brood parasitism, is common among cuckoos, and the koel is a member of the cuckoo family. Indian poets have written about its song for centuries.

{{story:crow-smart}}

Often you'll hear a bird long before you see it. Learning a few calls is like getting a second pair of eyes. The rising "ku-oo" of the koel, the bright whistle of the magpie robin and the harsh "kaa" of the crow are easy to start with.

Many apps and websites have recordings to compare. Some birders remember calls as little phrases, because many [[@birds-sing|birds sing]] in patterns that sound like words. In North America, the barred owl seems to ask "Who cooks for you?" Spend a few mornings just listening, and your city will start to sound very different.

{{story:migration}}

Many people's fear of sharks grew after a 1975 film called *Jaws*, about a giant great white that attacks swimmers. It was a huge hit. The writer of the novel it was based on, Peter Benchley, later said he regretted how it made people see sharks. He spent much of his life afterwards working to protect them.

Our brains are good at remembering scary stories and bad at judging risk. One shark bite on the news feels more real than thousands of safe swims. That's part of why [[@fear|fear]] of sharks is so much bigger than the danger.

{{story:fear}}

People have made sun-dried mud bricks for more than 9,000 years. Early farmers in West Asia shaped them by hand and stacked them into houses. Mud bricks keep homes cool in summer and warm in winter, and many villages still use them.

Fired bricks came later, and they changed cities. The people of the [[@indus-cities|Indus cities]], such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, used vast numbers of them for houses, drains and wells. Their bricks followed a standard shape, with thickness, width and length in the ratio 1:2:4. The same proportions turned up at sites hundreds of kilometres apart, showing careful planning. Much later, brick became an art material too. The terracotta temples of Bishnupur in West Bengal are covered in carved brick panels showing gods, battles and scenes of village life.

{{story:indus-cities}}

The standing trick is the easiest to understand. When you crouch at the top of the swing, your body is far from the top bar. As you rush down, you stand up straight, pulling yourself closer to the bar right at the bottom, where you are moving fastest.

Standing up there takes work, since your legs must push against both [[gravity|gravity]] and the swing's outward pull. That work goes into the swing as extra energy. Then you crouch again at the next high point, when moving costs almost nothing. Do it twice per full back-and-forth swing and you climb higher each time.

{{story:galileo}}

A plain radio wave carries no message. To add one, the transmitter changes the wave in a pattern that matches the sound. In AM radio, the height of the waves rises and falls. In FM radio, the waves bunch closer together and spread apart. The receiver reads those changes and rebuilds the sound.

Phones, [[@wifi|Wi-Fi]], TV, GPS and garage-door remotes all use radio waves too, each at its own set of frequencies. Governments divide the airwaves into bands so that one signal doesn't drown out another. Long waves bend around hills and can follow the curve of the Earth. Very short waves travel in straighter lines, which is why mobile towers need to be fairly close together and why an FM station fades as you drive away from the city.

{{story:voyager}}

A log rolls on its own, so the wheel can seem obvious. The tricky part is the axle, the rod the wheel turns around. The axle has to be perfectly round and smooth. The hole in the wheel must fit snugly but loosely enough to spin. Both must be strong enough to carry a heavy load over bumpy ground.

Making that fit needed good tools, and people needed animals to pull carts. Cattle had been tamed thousands of years earlier, but people still had to learn to yoke them to heavy loads. Copper tools, which made fine woodwork easier, were spreading at about the same time. Once all the pieces were ready, the wheel seems to have spread fast.

{{story:mesopotamia}}

उत्तर-पूर्वी चीन का हार्बिन हर सर्दी दुनिया का सबसे बड़ा बर्फ़ उत्सव मनाता है। वहाँ पारा अक्सर शून्य से 20 डिग्री नीचे चला जाता है। दिसंबर में मज़दूर जमी हुई सोंगहुआ नदी से विशाल सिल्लियाँ काटकर उत्सव के मैदान तक ढोते हैं।

फिर हज़ारों मज़दूर और कलाकार महल, मंदिर, फिसलपट्टियाँ और मीनारें बनाते हैं, जिनमें कुछ दस मंज़िला इमारत जितनी ऊँची होती हैं। रात में बर्फ़ के अंदर लगी रंगीन बत्तियाँ पूरी जगह को एक जगमगाते शहर में बदल देती हैं। पूरे चीन और दुनिया भर से लोग मोटे-मोटे कपड़ों में लिपटे आते हैं, और ठंड में फ़ोन की बैटरियाँ अक्सर लोगों से बहुत पहले जवाब दे देती हैं। जापान के सापोरो में भी एक मशहूर सर्दियों का उत्सव होता है, जहाँ दबाई हुई बर्फ़ (हिम) से विशाल मूर्तियाँ बनाई जाती हैं। बसंत आते-आते हार्बिन का शहर पिघलकर उसी नदी में लौट जाता है जहाँ से आया था।

{{story:why-ice-floats}}

Devendra Jhajharia lost his left arm after an electric shock when he was a boy. He took up the [[@sport-science-javelin|javelin]] and won Paralympic gold in Athens in 2004. Twelve years later, in Rio in 2016, he won gold again with a world record.

Sumit Antil lost part of his left leg in a road accident as a teenager. Throwing with a [[@prosthetics|prosthetic]] leg, he won gold at Tokyo and again at Paris, where he set a Paralympic record of 70.59 metres. That is longer than a cricket boundary on many grounds.

{{story:prosthetics}}

[[@heat-body|On a hot day]], or when you exercise, [[@body-belly-sounds|your skin flushes]] as tiny blood vessels near the surface widen. Warm blood flows close to the skin and gives off heat to the air.

Then you [[@sweat|sweat]]. Each drop takes heat with it as it evaporates. In dry heat, like a Rajasthan summer or an Arizona afternoon, sweat evaporates fast and cools you well. In humid weather, like Mumbai or Florida in the [[monsoon|monsoon]] season, sweat lingers on the skin and you feel sticky and hot.

{{story:heatwaves}}

जब अकेलापन महीनों तक चलता रहे, तो इसका असर मूड से आगे तक जा सकता है। जो लोग लंबे समय से अकेले हैं, वे अक्सर ठीक से नहीं सो पाते। उनका शरीर [[@stress|तनाव]] की हालत में बना रह सकता है, जिसमें वे [[hormone|हॉर्मोन]] ज़्यादा होते हैं जो हमें ख़तरे के लिए चौकन्ना रखते हैं।

विश्व स्वास्थ्य संगठन की 2025 की रिपोर्ट ने कहा कि लंबे समय का अकेलापन और दूसरों से कटे रहना दिल की बीमारी, डिप्रेशन और कम उम्र में मौत से जुड़ा है। उसका अनुमान था कि दुनिया भर में लगभग हर 6 में से 1 इंसान अकेलापन महसूस करता है। सरकारों ने इसे गंभीरता से लेना शुरू कर दिया है। ब्रिटेन ने 2018 में अकेलेपन के लिए एक मंत्री नियुक्त किया, और जापान ने 2021 में यही किया।

{{story:kindness-science}}

मीठा, नमकीन, खट्टा और कड़वा सदियों से पहचाने जाते रहे हैं। 1908 में जापानी रसायनशास्त्री किकुनाए इकेदा ने यह जानने की कोशिश की कि [[@seaweed-food|केल्प समुद्री शैवाल]] से बना शोरबा इतना ज़ायकेदार क्यों लगता है। उन्होंने पाया कि इसकी वजह ग्लूटामेट है, और इस स्वाद को उमामी नाम दिया, जिसका मोटा मतलब है "लज़ीज़ स्वाद"। यह टमाटर, चीज़, मशरूम और सोया सॉस में खूब होता है।

स्वाद कलिकाएँ ज़्यादातर जीभ के छोटे-छोटे उभारों पर बैठी होती हैं, कुछ तालू और गले में भी। तुम्हारे पास कुछ हज़ार हैं, और हर एक-दो हफ़्ते में ये बदल जाती हैं। वैज्ञानिक अभी जाँच रहे हैं कि क्या और भी बुनियादी स्वाद हो सकते हैं, जैसे वसा का एक स्वाद।

{{story:spicy-hot}}

Yoga began in ancient India as a spiritual practice. The Yoga Sutras, a collection of short teachings linked to the sage Patanjali, are roughly 1,600 to 2,000 years old. Scholars still argue about the exact date. They describe yoga mostly as training the mind through [[ethics|ethics]], breathing, concentration and meditation. Poses, called asanas, get only a few lines.

Long sequences of poses developed much later, especially in the last few hundred years. In the early 1900s, teachers in places like Mysore mixed old practices with gymnastics and exercise ideas. Swami Vivekananda had already introduced yoga's spiritual ideas to American audiences in the 1890s. Later Indian teachers took posture-based yoga to Europe and America, where it became hugely popular.

{{story:swami-vivekananda}}

In the Second World War, the German military used Enigma, a typewriter-like machine with spinning wheels that changed the code with every key pressed. The settings changed every day, giving an astronomical number of combinations.

Polish mathematicians, led by Marian Rejewski, first broke Enigma in 1932. They shared their work with Britain just before the war. At Bletchley Park, [[@alan-turing|Alan Turing]] and thousands of others built machines to speed up the search. Their work helped the Allies read many German messages.

{{story:prime-numbers}}

You see an object because light bounces off it into your [[@eyes-see|eyes]], or because it blocks light coming from behind. To be invisible, an object would have to let light carry on exactly as if it wasn't there, with no reflection, no shadow and no bending.

Glass is a good start, as it lets light through. But you can still see a glass because its edges bend light, which is the same effect that makes [[@lenses-glasses|lenses]] work. If you put a glass rod into oil that bends light by the same amount, the rod almost disappears.

{{story:octopus-colour}}

In September 1928, the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned to his laboratory at St Mary's Hospital in London after a summer holiday. He had left dishes of bacteria stacked on his bench. On one, a fuzzy blue-green mould had grown. Around it, the bacteria had died.

Fleming realised the mould was making something that killed germs. He called it penicillin. It took another 12 years, and the work of Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and their team at Oxford, to turn it into [[@medicine-how|a medicine]]. By the end of the Second World War, penicillin was saving countless soldiers from infected wounds. Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in 1945.

{{story:antibiotics}}

The gas hitting the first turbine blades can be hotter than the melting point of the metal they are made of. Engineers keep them working with three tricks. The blades are grown as single metal crystals, which makes them very strong. They are coated with a thin layer of heat-resistant ceramic. And cooler air is piped through hollow channels inside each blade and out through tiny holes, wrapping it in a thin protective film.

Each blade is about the size of your hand, but at full power it can feel a pull equal to the weight of a heavy truck as it spins.

{{story:rockets-how}}

After crawling, the search engine processes each page. It notes the words, where they appear, what language they're in, what the page is about, and much more. All this goes into the index.

The index works like the one at the back of a book. Instead of reading every page to find "cheetah", you look up "cheetah" and get a list of page numbers. A search engine's index holds hundreds of billions of pages and is far larger than any library. It is stored in huge buildings called [[@data-centres|data centres]], packed with computers.

{{story:dictionaries}}

Old Japanese records describe shinobi slipping into enemy castles at night, sometimes during a siege. They might start fires to cause confusion, or open a gate. More often, they simply watched and listened. Their kit was simple and practical: a rope with a hook, a folding saw, a small cloth for filtering water and a tiny bag of medicine. Many tools doubled as farm tools, so a ninja searched at a checkpoint looked like any other villager. Even the famous sickle-and-chain weapon began as a farmer's sickle.

Disguise was everything. A ninja might travel as a wandering monk, an entertainer, a merchant or a farmer. He would learn the local way of speaking and the prices in the market. A manual called the *Bansenshukai*, written in 1676, gives advice on disguises, signals, climbing walls and making fire, and even notes on reading the weather and the stars.

{{story:spies-history}}

The first fleet left in 1405. Ming records say it had about 317 ships and 27,800 people: sailors, soldiers, doctors, translators, astrologers and officials. It was like a floating town.

The ships were built at a great shipyard near Nanjing. They had many masts, watertight compartments inside the hull, and huge rudders. Sailors steered with magnetic compasses and star charts, and timed their voyages with the [[@monsoon|monsoon]] winds, which blow towards India in summer and back in winter.

{{story:monsoon}}

About 165 million years ago, Madagascar and India, still joined together, broke away from Africa as the old [[@pangaea|supercontinent]] of [[gondwana|Gondwana]] split apart. Then, around 88 million years ago, India separated from Madagascar and began [[@india-drift|drifting north]] towards Asia, where it later crashed in and pushed up the Himalaya.

Madagascar stayed behind, sitting about 400 kilometres off Africa. Any new animals had to cross the sea. Only a few kinds of land [[mammal|mammal]] ever made it, probably by floating over on rafts of fallen trees after storms. Each lucky group then spread out and evolved into many new species, filling jobs that monkeys, cats or [[@woodpeckers|woodpeckers]] do elsewhere.

{{story:continental-drift}}

Next, pilgrims walk seven times between two small hills, Safa and Marwah, now inside the mosque. This remembers Hajar running between them searching for water for her baby, Ismail. According to tradition, water then sprang from the ground, and the Zamzam well still flows today. Pilgrims drink from it and often carry bottles home.

On the most important day, pilgrims gather on the plain of Arafat, praying and asking forgiveness from noon until sunset. They spend the night under the open sky at Muzdalifah, collecting small pebbles. Over the next days, at Mina, they throw the pebbles at three stone pillars, representing the rejection of evil.

{{story:ibn-battuta}}

Mustard is a rabi crop, sown after the [[monsoon|monsoon]] in October and November and harvested in February and March. It needs little water, so it suits dry places. Rajasthan grows nearly half of India's mustard, followed by states such as Madhya Pradesh, Haryana and Uttar Pradesh.

When the plants flower, whole districts turn yellow. Bees buzz between the blooms, and farmers often keep beehives at the edges of fields, because [[@pollinators|pollinators]] help the seed pods fill. One of the most famous scenes in Hindi cinema, from the 1995 film *Dilwale Dulhania Le Jayenge*, is a song set among flowering mustard fields in Punjab.

{{story:pollinators}}
